    "id": "thm:restricted-uniform-oracle-search-answer",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "Let \\(m=|\\mathcal A|\\) and \\(L=|\\mathrm{LIT}|\\).  Exact minimum-cost optimization over the restricted uniform acquisition certificates \\(E\\) accepted by \\(\\operatorname{Acc}\\) has a deterministic algorithm that makes at most \\(2^m\\) distinct queries to \\(\\mathcal O_{\\mathrm{src}}\\), runs in\n\\[\n 2^{O(m+L)}\\operatorname{poly}(N_{\\mathrm{in}}+N_{\\mathcal O}),\n\\]\nand uses\n\\[\n 2^{O(m+L)}\\operatorname{poly}(N_{\\mathrm{in}}+N_{\\mathcal O})\n\\]\noracle-answer space.  It returns the lexicographically first optimum restricted uniform certificate when \\(\\operatorname{Opt}<+\\infty\\), and otherwise certifies \\(\\operatorname{Opt}=+\\infty\\).  Thus the positive algorithmic alternative in the oracle-search question holds for the explicitly restricted uniform-certificate problem; no oracle lower bound is asserted.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "A",
      "LIT",
      "Ecert",
      "Acc",
      "Osrc",
      "Nin",
      "Noracle",
      "Opt"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "thm:explicit-oracle-enumeration-frontier"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Put \\(m=|\\mathcal A|\\) and \\(L=|\\mathrm{LIT}|\\).  Theorem~\\(\\mathrm{Explicit\\ oracle\\ enumeration\\ frontier}\\) supplies a deterministic algorithm for the uniform certificates accepted by \\(\\operatorname{Acc}\\).  These are exactly the restricted uniform certificates quantified over in the present statement; the word ``restricted'' records that scope and does not change the feasible set.  The cited theorem gives, simultaneously,\n\\[\n \\#\\{\\text{distinct queries to }\\mathcal O_{\\mathrm{src}}\\}\\leq 2^m,\n\\]\n\\[\n T,\\;S_{\\mathrm{oracle}}\n \\leq 2^{O(m+L)}\\operatorname{poly}(N_{\\mathrm{in}}+N_{\\mathcal O}),\n\\]\nwhere \\(T\\) is running time and \\(S_{\\mathrm{oracle}}\\) is oracle-answer space.  It also states that the returned certificate is the lexicographically first optimum when \\(\\operatorname{Opt}<+\\infty\\), whereas a final infinite value certifies \\(\\operatorname{Opt}=+\\infty\\).  These are every algorithmic and output clause of the present theorem.  Consequently the positive alternative in the oracle-search question holds for the restricted uniform problem.  Since the invoked theorem asserts only this upper bound, the corollary asserts no oracle lower bound.",
